From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 07:19:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(open-items): keep a reply thread on each item, created
 only by the tool

An item is meant to work like a comment thread on a document: created by
one action, discussed inside it, and resolved. The store kept only the
user's answers, the agent's reply to a comment existed only as chat text
that the web had to find again, and a raise could adopt any label the
agent had already written in chat, so a label made up in the text could
still become an item.

- sys_open_items_update takes `reply`, the agent's answer on the item's
  thread; on its own it never closes the item, and with status closed and
  a resolution it answers and settles in one call. People reply through
  PATCH or `POST .../open-items/replies` (renamed from `/answers`).
- Each item keeps `replies` (author user or agent, text, time, and who
  for a person), the latest ten that fit its stored size. A reply carries
  the write that made it (`ref`: the tool call, or the web's comment
  submission), so a retry adds it once. The user replying makes an open
  item answered; the agent replying hands an answered item back as open.
- A raise no longer takes `label`; the server allocates every label and
  refuses an item that names one. The tool descriptions say that raising
  is the only way to create an item and that items and replies show to
  the user. sys_open_items_list shows the latest three replies.
- Stored labels can still be given by an import of chat labels, which is
  the only caller of a raise with a number.

Not proposed upstream yet.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
